Revision history for skilletor

{{$NEXT}}
  - Five oddities the Perl port found are fixed. A source serving two foreign
    entries of one bundle asks for trust once, not twice. A `\U` escape above
    U+10FFFF in a bundle file is that bundle's warning instead of an error
    that aborted the whole source scan. A `SKILL.md`, agent or rule with CRLF
    line ends or a BOM keeps its description in the catalog. An install key
    only `Object.prototype` knows (`constructor`, `__proto__`) is an unknown
    type, and a spec such as `constructor/foo` is no forge link.
    `uninstall` no longer throws on a non-string entry in an install list.
    Conformance cases 13 to 16 pin them for both engines.
  - A `url` source serving a tarball with a corrupt header size no longer
    hangs a sync: the update fails as a corrupt archive and keeps the last
    good cache, with a warning.
  - `conformance/` holds cases (config, source trees, the expected
    resolution, and for sync cases the files, lock, check and status) that
    the TypeScript engine and the Perl port both run;
    `SKILLETOR_CONFORMANCE_UPDATE=1` regenerates them from the TypeScript
    reference. Cases cover removal, conflicts and `--force`, check drift,
    `"gitignore": false`, briefing, settings.json and executable bits;
    fixture names starting with `.git` are stored as `dot.git...` so a
    checkout holds every fixture.
  - `install.sh` sets skilletor up from a plain shell (GitHub issue #1):
    `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Getty/skilletor/main/install.sh | sh`
    installs the plugin when `claude` is on `PATH` (or updates it) and writes
    a `~/.local/bin/skilletor` that survives plugin updates; without Claude
    Code, or with `--no-plugin`, it clones the repository and links
    `bin/skilletor`. `--bin-dir` picks the directory. The README has it under
    "From the command line"; the Hermes docs name the command `SKILLETOR_BIN`.

0.7.0     2026-10-16 20:00:22Z
  - The plugin, package and README descriptions name Hermes Agent as the third
    target.
  - The Hermes plugin installs with Hermes' own installer:
    `hermes plugins install Getty/skilletor/hermes/skilletor --enable`, then
    `hermes plugins update skilletor`. It does not bring the CLI: name a clone's
    `bin/skilletor` as `SKILLETOR_BIN` in `$HERMES_HOME/.env`, or put
    `skilletor` on `PATH`; Hermes shows how after the install. The hand-linked
    install still runs the checkout's CLI. The bundled skill states this setup
    and that `["hermes"]` alone in `targets` drops Claude and Codex.
  - `bin/skilletor` follows its own symlinks to find `dist/`, so a link on
    `PATH` (`ln -s <clone>/bin/skilletor ~/.local/bin/skilletor`), `npm link`
    and `npm install -g` run the CLI instead of failing with "Cannot find
    module".
  - The bundled skill is shorter, with source, Hermes and sync detail in its
    references.
  - A project sync makes the plugin a requirement of the project: in the
    committed `.claude/settings.json` it enables `skilletor@<marketplace>`,
    the marketplace Claude Code installed skilletor from, and declares in
    `extraKnownMarketplaces` every marketplace an `enabledPlugins` entry there
    needs (`briefing@getty` too), so a fresh checkout offers to install them.
    The report says what it added and asks to commit the file once. It only
    adds: existing keys and the file's formatting stay, `false` entries are
    respected, a marketplace with a source on this machine's disk is never
    written, and a file that does not parse is left alone with a warning. It
    runs for projects with a committed `skilletor.json` and Claude Code as a
    target; `"claudeSettings": false` turns it off. A config with
    `claudeSettings` is a config error for skilletor 0.6.0 and earlier. The
    README keeps the snippet for the standalone CLI under "Sharing a project:
    require the plugin". Codex has no equivalent.

0.6.0     2026-10-15 17:39:19Z
  - Hermes Agent is a third target, `hermes` in `targets`. It is opt-in:
    never detected, so `"targets"` in `~/.claude/skilletor.json` must name it,
    together with every other harness in use (`["claude", "hermes"]`). Skills
    install into the Hermes home (`$HERMES_HOME`, else Hermes' own default:
    `~/.hermes`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\hermes` on Windows) and, in a project, into
    `.hermes/skills`; agents and rules are reported as not installed. The
    plugin in `hermes/skilletor` runs skilletor's hooks when a Hermes session
    starts and on each turn, reports changes to the model, says how to opt in
    while `hermes` is not in `targets`, and fails open. On Windows it starts
    `bin/skilletor.exe`.
  - A source entry takes an optional `path`, a directory or a list of them
    relative to the source root, for repositories that keep skills outside
    `skills/<name>/`, such as `"path": ["skills", "optional-skills"]` for
    NousResearch/hermes-agent. Every directory beneath it that holds a
    `SKILL.md` is one skill, and the source then offers those skills only. A
    name found in two directories is an error of that skill alone. A config
    with `path` is a config error for skilletor 0.4.2 and earlier.
    Skills a sync installed there (carrying skilletor's `.gitignore`) are
    not offered, so a `path` into a `.claude/skills` offers the repo's own.
    Names that differ only in case count as one name on every system, as
    they share a directory on macOS and Windows.
  - `skilletor add` of a GitHub link into a directory (`.../tree/<ref>/<dir>`)
    sets `ref` and `path` instead of being refused.
  - Agent worktrees get the project's installed skills, agents and rules. A
    project that uses skilletor gets a fixed block in `.worktreeinclude`
    naming the paths skilletor installs to, its lock and
    `skilletor.local.json`; Claude Code copies the gitignored files it matches
    into every worktree it creates (`--worktree`, subagents with `isolation:
    worktree`, desktop parallel sessions). Commit the file once, when the
    report asks; your own lines in it stay untouched. A project synced before
    gets the block in its next session.
  - Windows: the background sync a prompt starts runs with its window
    hidden, so no console window can flash up.
  - A source entry needs no `git`, `url` or `local` of its own when another
    config file gives that source one: `{ "ref": "v2" }` or `{ "path": [...] }`
    in `skilletor.local.json` changes only that field of the project's source.
    A source no file gives a backend is a config error naming the source and
    each file declaring it. A user config entry without one changes projects
    only; a user install naming it is a config error.
  - `skilletor add` of a forge link: a `?query` or `#anchor` is dropped, also
    from a repository link; `/releases/tag/<tag>` and `/commit/<sha>` pin that
    ref; `%`-escapes in a link's ref and path are decoded (`%2F` puts a `/`
    into a ref); `Getty/karr/tree/v1` and `github:Getty/karr/tree/v1` pin the
    ref like `github.com/...` instead of dropping it. GitLab (`/-/tree/`,
    `/-/commit/`, `/-/tags/`), Codeberg (`/src/branch|tag|commit/`,
    `/releases/tag/`, `/commit/`) and Hugging Face (`/tree/`, `/commit/`,
    `datasets/` and `spaces/` repositories) links are read the same way, and
    a GitLab project path keeps every segment before `/-/` (nested groups).
    Any other page (`/issues`, `/pulls`, `/releases`, a segment past the
    repository such as `Getty/karr/extra`) is an error naming the repository
    to add, instead of a source that tracked HEAD or failed at fetch.
  - Names that differ only in case count as one name everywhere, not only
    under a source's `path`: `skills/Foo` beside `skills/foo` (or
    `agents/Bar.md` beside `agents/bar.md.njk`) in a source, in `.claude/`
    and in `plugin.json` skills are the duplicate-name error; `Foo@a` and
    `foo@b` in one install list are a duplicate target; two sources' wildcards
    offering `Foo` and `foo` skip both with a warning, and an explicit entry
    wins over another source's other spelling.
  - macOS, Windows: an item whose name changes only in case (`Foo@a` to
    `foo@a`, a source renaming `skills/Foo`), or a file inside an item
    renamed so, is no longer a conflict with its own old copy nor deleted
    with the old spelling after the sync wrote it: the file both spellings
    name is taken over (reported as added and removed, as on Linux).
    `uninstall foo@a` removes an entry `Foo@a`, and names the wildcard or
    bundle that installed `Foo`.
  - `"ref": null` in the user config or `skilletor.local.json` unpins a
    source: the `ref` a lower config file gave is dropped and `git` tracks
    the remote's HEAD again.
  - `skilletor source remove` refuses to remove the entry giving a source its
    only `git`, `url` or `local` while another config file keeps an entry for
    it without one, which would leave a config that no longer loads; the error
    names those files and how to remove them.

0.4.2     2026-10-06 05:28:16Z
  - Windows: hooks and the CLI start without Git Bash. `bin/skilletor.exe`, a
    small launcher next to the sh script, runs the bundle with the first
    `node.exe` on `PATH` and is the `skilletor` command in PowerShell and cmd.
  - Windows: a project synced there and on Linux gets the same files and the
    same lock. Sources are checked out with the repository's line endings,
    whatever `core.autocrlf` says, and paths inside an item, a lock key or a
    `.gitignore` line use `/`.
  - The Claude Code hooks are declared in exec form and start `bin/skilletor`
    without a shell.
  - A source name outside `^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$` is a config error.
  - Specs: an empty spec, a blank `github:` or forge owner, a shorthand owner,
    repo or port no forge takes, and an unknown `word:` prefix are errors. A
    scheme and `github:` match in any case, `.TAR.GZ` is a url, an empty repo
    segment in `github:` means the default repo, and git's
    `transport::address` form is a git source kept as written. A GitHub tree
    link is the repo at that ref; a file or subdirectory link is an error.
  - `add` refuses a non-https url source before writing anything, and refuses
    to keep an entry the config loader rejects, before trusting it.
  - `source remove`: a name the target config lacks is an error that names
    where else it is declared; an entry the config loader rejects can be
    removed when nothing else is refused.
  - Bundles: an entry address the config loader rejects is a bundle error, not
    a missing source, and a bundle's missing source never replaces one in the
    target config.
  - Catalog: `x.md` beside `x.md.njk` is an error of that agent or rule, and
    `f` beside `f.njk` inside a skill is an error of that skill.
  - `add`, `install`, `uninstall` and `source remove` exit 2 when their sync
    hits a config error.
  - A skill's scripts keep their executable bit. After the upgrade, one sync
    gives earlier installs and url caches theirs.

0.4.1     2026-09-26 22:58:47Z
  - First published release. The versions before it, 0.1.0 to 0.4.0, were
    development builds listed in the marketplace.
